// Day 22: Coverage check before hunting for inbox rule abuse (ATT&CK T1564.008)
//
// Hypothesis B: "If a user account was phished and accessed by an attacker, the attacker would
// create an inbox rule that moves or deletes security or reply emails."
//
// What it looks for: whether CloudAppEvents has any rows at all. Inbox rule creation would show
// up here as Exchange mailbox activity, so an empty table means the hypothesis cannot be tested,
// not that it was disproved. Expanding a row from take 10 also shows every available field,
// which is how I start building a real query against an unfamiliar table.
//
// Result in my lab: 0 rows over the last 30 days. Exchange activity was not reaching Advanced
// Hunting, so Hypothesis B was untestable in this tenant.
//
// What it would take to test it: Exchange mailbox audit events flowing into CloudAppEvents
// (Microsoft 365 app connector in Defender for Cloud Apps) or into Sentinel's OfficeActivity
// table (Office 365 connector), then an inbox rule created in Outlook on the web by a lab user,
// then a rerun after the ingestion delay. Defender for Cloud Apps anomaly detection for
// suspicious inbox manipulation rules would be a stronger control than a hunting query.
CloudAppEvents
| take 10
